  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "A+rw8mue5MpmmvyJp33nPPhd5Hk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:00.078Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The legacy inline format is not deprecated, it is **removed**. The document records Phase 3 as done: the `attachments` field on `Action` and the inline `AttachmentInput` / `Attachment` types (`{ data: string /* base64 */, mimeType, extension?, fileName? }`) no longer exist, and all attachment data flows through `IAttachmentService`, `IAttachmentStore` and `IAttachmentTransport`.\n\nThe problems that motivated the removal are worth keeping, because they are the argument for refs in any similar design: operations carried the full binary payload through the job queue, the sync channels and GraphQL transport; large files bloated the operation store and sync bandwidth permanently, since operations are immutable; an attachment could not be uploaded independently of submitting an action; and there was no lifecycle at all \u2014 an attachment either existed inline or did not.\n\nPhase 2 completed the sync half: channels no longer transfer inline attachment data, bytes move via the transport (lazy fetch or eager push), and transport configuration became part of the remote/channel configuration.\n\nFor a model author migrating an old schema, the mechanical change is to replace `data: String` (or a composite `{ image, imageMediaType, imageUrl }`) with a single `attachment: AttachmentRef` on both the input and the state type.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments.md` \u2014 Current State; Migration from Inline Attachments (Phases 1-3)\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:20:00.078Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:00.078Z"
    }
  },
